Yes, the code above has successfully implemented each of the suggested aliases for your Bash script. As you may recall, the Bash script is intended to perform tasks using various command-line tools, and these aliases will allow you to quickly and easily access these commands without having to manually type in their full names. 

For example, to view all available commands:
```bash
$ ./script >> ~/Desktop/script_log.txt 2>&1
# If using terminal app (such as Terminal)
sudo systemctl start omniscient >> ~/Desktop/script_log.txt 2>&1
# If using Docker container with OmniSciDB database installed
```

To show the aliases, you can run this command in the Bash script's terminal session:
```bash
$ bash -c "echo -e '
Omniscient Aliases:
   omnictl
   omnigui
   omniscient
   omnistarthttps://omnisci.com/' >> ~/Desktop/script_log.txt"
```
This command will append the full names of the aliases to your log file, making it easier for you to quickly identify and access each alias. You can then run this command repeatedly to keep track of all available commands in your Bash script.
